  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Sixty. Forty is the count in each of the other three UTME subjects, and fifty and eighty are inventions.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Ten, one sixth of the paper. Five is the comprehension passage, fifteen would swallow the whole of Section A, and twenty is double the truth.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    *The Lekki Headmaster* by Kabir Alabi Garba. *The Life Changer* is the text it replaced, *Sweet Sixteen* was prescribed years earlier, and *The Village Headmaster* is the old television drama the nickname echoes.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Kabir Alabi Garba. The other three wrote *The Life Changer*, *Sweet Sixteen* and *Things Fall Apart* respectively — all real authors, none of them this book's.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Twelve, from *Dusk* to *…Dawn*. Every other number is close enough to feel familiar, which is what makes this a memory question and not a reading one.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Mr Adebepo Adewale, known as Bepo. Egi Meko directs Beesway, Jeremi Amos keeps Stardom's accounts, and Ope Wande teaches physics and preaches.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Stardom Schools, Lekki. Beesway is the school he left, Fruitful Future is the school he co-founded, and Stardom Kiddies is the nursery and primary arm where he was headmaster first.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Lekki, in Lagos State — the title says so. Ibadan is where the passport office is, and Ikeja and Abuja are never the setting.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Seri. Nike and Kike are the couple's two children, and Apeh is the vice principal's surname.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Nike and Kike. Favour is a pupil at Stardom, the daughter at the centre of the paternity result in Chapter 4.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    A nurse, earning about £10,000 a month. *Teacher* is the trap: the novel gives the teaching job to Bepo, not to his wife.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    ₦400,000 a month. ₦175,000 is what the English teacher Fafore earns, and the other two figures are inventions.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Mrs Ibidun Gloss. Apeh is the vice principal, Solape Bayo chairs the board, and Beke Egbin is the guidance counsellor.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Mrs Grace Apeh, who takes charge at the assembly. Titi is the school nurse and Beke Egbin the counsellor — both real posts, both wrong here.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    A fact question: one clause in one chapter. Sequence asks for order, character asks for a trait, and technique asks what a scene or a title is doing.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    A sequence question, answered from the timeline rather than from the card. The other three shapes do not turn on order.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    A character question, and it must be answered from an action — an adjective with no deed behind it is a guess.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    The true irrelevance. The wrong name attaches a real character to the wrong deed, the half-truth is right only in its first clause, and the overstatement uses an absolute.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    The half-truth, and it is the most expensive of the five shapes because the eye stops at the comma. Read every option to its full stop.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    An overstatement. A novel about compromise rarely supports an absolute, and one counter-example in the text kills the option.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    The wrong name. It is the commonest lie in a fact question, because every name in the option is a name the candidate recognises.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    The wrong chapter. The cure is to place the event on the timeline before looking at the options.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    First. Ten questions answered from memory in about four minutes; doing them early banks the cheapest marks while the clock is friendly.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    About four minutes — roughly twenty-five seconds each. Ten minutes is a quarter of the paper spent on one sixth of the questions.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Four: what happens, who is there, what it shows, and one line worth keeping. The last two answer character and theme questions.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    *What it shows.* The quotation column supports a style question, and the first two columns carry the fact questions.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Straight through. Notes on a first read slow the story down and produce a file nobody can use; the cards belong to the second read.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    With the cards open, one card per chapter. It is the only read that produces anything you can revise from.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    About twenty minutes. That is the whole point of the file: the text, compressed to something you can re-read in the week of the examination.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Flag it and move. The names in later questions often unlock an earlier one, and the clock is worth more than the stubbornness.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    No negative marking. A blank therefore scores nothing while a guess scores a quarter of a mark in expectation.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    A quarter of a mark — the value of the guess that was not made. Across sixty questions that is up to fifteen marks.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Third person, limited to Bepo. The pronoun is *he*, and the reader is told only what Bepo knows — which is why the judgements feel like facts.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    *Dusk*. *Dawn* is the last chapter, and the two titles together frame the book.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    *…Dawn*, with the ellipsis before it. *Point of No Return* is the farewell in Chapter 11, and *Snake in the Roof* is Chapter 5.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Chapter 10, *Passport Pains*. Chapter 5 is the cooperative, Chapter 8 is the unfinished business, and Chapter 12 is the departure.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Chapter 6, *Ade as Well as Jide COMES vs. COME*. Chapter 7 is the ritual at Beesway and Chapter 9 is the excursion.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    One sixth — ten of sixty questions. One third would be twenty questions, which is twice the block.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    The cards hold exactly what the questions ask for, in a form that can be revised in twenty minutes. The other three are not reasons a candidate should act on.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 6.56pt)[
    Section A. The novel sits beside the comprehension and cloze passages, which is why a candidate meets it early on the screen.
  ]

]

